# OverTheWire Bandit — Complete Walkthrough

A personal study repository documenting my completion of the **OverTheWire Bandit** wargame.

> **Scope:** This repository is for the OverTheWire Bandit training environment. Do not reuse scanning, brute-force, or exploitation techniques against systems without authorization.

## What Bandit teaches

Bandit is designed for beginners and builds command-line/Linux fundamentals through progressive levels. The official site describes it as a beginner-focused wargame and recommends using `man`, `help`, and notes while solving levels.

## Contents

- `levels/` — one Markdown file per transition
- `WALKTHROUGH.md` — compact complete walkthrough
- `NOTES.md` — lessons learned / skills map
- `LICENSE` — repository documentation
- `.gitignore` — keeps local secrets out of Git

## Important: passwords are intentionally NOT stored here

Passwords change and are credentials for the live training environment. I intentionally excluded the password list from this public repository.

The screenshot from my completion proof has been sanitized separately with the passwords blurred.

## Connection

```bash
ssh bandit<LEVEL>@bandit.labs.overthewire.org -p 2220
```

Start at Level 0 and use the password obtained from each level to authenticate to the next one.

## Source

Official OverTheWire Bandit:
https://overthewire.org/wargames/bandit/

The individual level goals and command suggestions in this repository are based on the official level pages, with the commands presented as a concise study-oriented solution path.

## Skills practiced

- SSH
- Linux filesystem navigation
- File permissions and ownership
- `find`, `grep`, `sort`, `uniq`, `strings`
- Base64 and ROT13
- Hexdump reversal
- gzip / bzip2 / tar
- SSH keys
- TCP sockets and TLS
- Nmap service discovery
- Cron
- Setuid
- Shell behavior and restricted shells
- Git branches, history, tags, commits and push
